  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of the causes of the bladder not emptying completely (a condition known as **urinary retention**):\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udee7 Why Your Bladder May Not Empty Completely\n\nUrinary retention is a condition where your bladder doesn't empty all the way or at all when you urinate. There are several possible causes, which can be grouped into categories:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udd32 Blockages / Obstructions\nIn some cases, it can be due to an obstruction making it more difficult to empty your bladder. This obstruction can be caused by an **enlarged prostate** in men, a **kidney stone** blocking the urethra, **constipation**, or a **stricture (narrowing) of the urethra**, which makes it difficult for urine to flow out.\n\n- **In men specifically:** Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is the most common cause of urinary retention in males. The bladder is weakened when the prostate gland \u2014 which sits below the bladder and wraps around the urethra \u2014 is enlarged, causing you to lose the ability to empty the bladder completely.\n\n- **In women specifically:** Pelvic floor dysfunction due to genetics, age, obesity, or childbirth is a common cause of urinary retention in females. This may include a prolapsed bladder (a cystocele), a common type of pelvic organ prolapse that can cause obstruction of urinary flow, where supportive tissues in the pelvic floor are weakened and can no longer support the bladder, causing it to drop into the vaginal canal.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83d\udcaa Muscle Problems\nIncomplete bladder emptying occurs when the muscles of the bladder are not able to squeeze properly to empty the bladder.\n\nThis might happen due to pelvic floor muscle tension (hypertonic muscles) that restricts the natural flow of urine, weakened pelvic floor muscles that can't provide proper support for bladder function, poor coordination between pelvic floor muscles and bladder muscles during urination, or weakness in the bladder muscle itself (the detrusor muscle).\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83e\udde0 Nerve Damage / Neurological Conditions\nThis can happen if there has been nerve or muscle damage, perhaps caused by an injury, surgery, or diseases such as **Parkinson's disease**, **multiple sclerosis**, and **spina bifida**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udc8a Medications\nCertain medications taken for other conditions, including **antihistamines**, **antispasmodics**, **opiates**, and **tricyclic antidepressants**, can impact the nerves that signal the bladder muscle to contract, resulting in decreased function.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83d\udebd Constipation\nPressure on the bladder from a large amount of stool in the colon can cause the bladder to not contract as it should or empty well.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83e\udda0 Infections\nUrinary tract infections (UTIs) and sexually transmitted infections (STIs) \u2014 inflammation and infection of the urinary tract and genitals \u2014 can lead to urinary retention.\n\n---\n\n### 7. \ud83d\udd2e Other Structural Issues\nKidney stones can crystallize and create an obstruction that blocks the flow of urine. Large uterine fibroids may also press against one of the ureters, causing bladder issues.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f What Happens If Left Untreated?\nIf you are not able to empty completely, your bladder and its muscles may become floppy over time. With larger amounts of urine being held in the bladder all the time, urine will leak out when you don't want it to and you may have a constant feeling of fullness.\n\n---\n\n> **Note:** If you are experiencing difficulty emptying your bladder, it is important to see a doctor. They can identify the underlying cause and recommend the appropriate treatment.",
